From: MediaBox
Subject: v4l2convert: interpose the fortified spellings of open()

v4l2convert.so is an LD_PRELOAD wrapper: it exists so that an application which
was never linked against libv4l2 still reaches a libv4l plugin when it opens
/dev/video*. It interposes `open` and `open64`.

A hardened build calls neither.

With _FORTIFY_SOURCE set -- Debian's default for every package, and what the
Chromium on this appliance is built with -- glibc's <fcntl.h> rewrites
open(path, flags) into __open_2(path, flags) whenever `flags` is a compile-time
constant that does not contain O_CREAT, because then there is no mode argument
left to check. `readelf -d` on the browser shows exactly that: it imports
__open_2 and __open64_2 beside open and open64, and Chromium's V4L2 probe
passes a constant O_RDWR|O_NONBLOCK|O_CLOEXEC.

Measured on RK3588 before this patch: strace shows the browser opening
/dev/video0 and walking /dev/video1..255, so the enumeration is happening and
the node is found -- and the plugin behind it, with its logging turned all the
way up, prints nothing at all, because the call went straight to glibc. From
the outside this is indistinguishable from a device that answered wrongly.

So the two fortified entry points are added. glibc emits them only after
proving O_CREAT is absent, which is why neither forwards a mode: each hands the
path to the wrapper's own open() and the ordinary path takes over from there.

Not added: __openat_2 and __openat64_2. This file does not interpose openat()
either, and adding half of that pair would mean a wrapper whose behaviour
depended on which spelling a caller happened to compile to. The V4L2 probe uses
open().
--- a/lib/libv4l2/v4l2convert.c
+++ b/lib/libv4l2/v4l2convert.c
@@ -87,7 +87,32 @@
 	return fd;
 }
 
+/*
+ * The fortified spellings of open(), which are the ones a hardened build
+ * actually calls.
+ *
+ * With _FORTIFY_SOURCE on -- Debian's default, and what Chromium is built with
+ * -- glibc's <fcntl.h> rewrites open(path, flags) into __open_2(path, flags)
+ * whenever `flags` is a compile-time constant with no O_CREAT, because then
+ * there is no mode argument to check. The call never reaches the `open` symbol
+ * at all, so an LD_PRELOAD that interposes only `open` interposes nothing:
+ * measured on RK3588, where Chromium opened /dev/video0 straight through this
+ * wrapper and the V4L2 plugin behind it was never asked a single question.
+ *
+ * glibc emits these only after proving O_CREAT is absent, so there is no mode
+ * to forward and open() below takes the ordinary path.
+ */
+LIBV4L_PUBLIC int __open_2(const char *file, int oflag)
+{
+	return open(file, oflag);
+}
+
 #if defined(linux) && defined(__GLIBC__)
+LIBV4L_PUBLIC int __open64_2(const char *file, int oflag)
+{
+	return open(file, oflag | O_LARGEFILE);
+}
+
 LIBV4L_PUBLIC int open64(const char *file, int oflag, ...)
 {
 	int fd;
